.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt CAPGET 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm capget ,
.Nm capset
.Nd read or narrow the calling thread's capabilities
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft long
.Fn syscall SYS_capget "void *hdrp" "void *datap"
.Ft long
.Fn syscall SYS_capset "void *hdrp" "const void *datap"
.Pp
musl has no wrappers for the two calls.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Capabilities are the privileges Linux divides the power of user ID 0
into, numbered 0 to 40.
A thread whose effective user ID is 0 has every capability it has not
given up, and any other thread none.
The kernel keeps one set for each thread as its effective and permitted
sets, and keeps its inheritable and ambient sets as Linux does.
The ambient set, which
.Dv PR_CAP_AMBIENT
of
.Xr prctl 2
changes, holds only capabilities that are both permitted and
inheritable.
What only a privileged thread may do, the kernel allows a thread that has
.Dv CAP_SYS_ADMIN
(21), where Linux asks for the one capability each operation needs; a
thread without it is refused, whatever its user IDs.
.Dv CAP_SETPCAP
(8) also allows a drop from the bounding set
.Pq see Xr prctl 2 ;
the other capabilities guard nothing of their own.
.Pp
The header gives the version of the structures and a thread:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct __user_cap_header_struct {
	uint32_t version;
	int pid;
};

struct __user_cap_data_struct {
	uint32_t effective;
	uint32_t permitted;
	uint32_t inheritable;
};
.Ed
.Pp
.Dv _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_1 ,
0x19980330, has one data structure, for capabilities 0 to 31.
.Dv _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_2
and
.Dv _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_3 ,
0x20071026 and 0x20080522, have two, the second for capabilities 32 to 40.
.Pp
.Fn capget
stores the sets of the thread whose ID the header gives, or of the
caller for 0: every capability in the effective and permitted sets of a
thread that has them, none in those of one that does not, and the
inheritable set the thread keeps.
With a null
.Fa datap
it checks the version alone.
.Pp
.Fn capset
changes the caller's sets: the thread keeps the capabilities of the
permitted set given and loses the rest.
The permitted set may hold only capabilities the thread has, and the
effective set only those of the permitted set; the thread then holds the
permitted set as both.
The inheritable set may add to the one the thread has only capabilities
of the bounding set, and, for a thread without
.Dv CAP_SETPCAP ,
only those it holds.
The ambient set keeps only the capabilities in both new sets.
Bits past capability 40 are ignored.
A version-1 header, whose sets have no second word, gives up capabilities
32 to 40.
Chromium's zygote gives up every capability of each process it forks, and
keeps
.Dv CAP_SYS_ADMIN
alone in a user namespace; bubblewrap keeps those its caller asks for,
permitted, effective and inheritable, and raises them as ambient.
.Pp
The bounding set limits what an exec gives.
Each process starts with every capability in it;
.Dv PR_CAPBSET_DROP
of
.Xr prctl 2
takes one out for good, and a fork passes it on.
A new user namespace, or one joined, starts the bounding set and the
capabilities whole again, and the inheritable and ambient sets empty, as
on Linux.
.Pp
A fork passes the capabilities and the four sets on.
An exec gives a thread that gave up capabilities every capability of its
bounding and inheritable sets again, when its user IDs make it root, as
on Linux, unless no_new_privs is set, the thread has a tracer that is not
privileged, or another process shares its directories; the thread then
keeps what it had.
A capability in neither set is not given by an exec, and a thread that
holds it keeps it until it gives it up.
An exec keeps the inheritable set.
It keeps the ambient set for an image that runs without set-user-ID or
set-group-ID bits, and otherwise empties it.
An image whose user IDs make it root holds those capabilities already;
one whose user IDs do not holds the capabilities of the ambient set,
effective and permitted, and no other, as on Linux, until its next exec
or until its user IDs leave root.
A
.Xr setuid 2 ,
.Xr setreuid 2
or
.Xr setresuid 2
that leaves none of the three user IDs root empties it too, as on Linux.
The tracer is judged at the exec, by every live thread of the tracing
process; Linux judges the credentials the attaching thread had when it
attached.
An exec that gives capabilities back leaves the new image not dumpable and
clears the parent-death signal, as an exec that changes the effective
user ID does.
A root thread without
.Dv CAP_SYS_ADMIN
may not attach to a thread of another process that has it, nor open its
memory through
.Pa /proc ,
and a thread that has it may not make its tracer, with
.Dv PTRACE_TRACEME ,
a parent process with a live thread that lacks it
.Pq see Xr ptrace 2 .
The target is judged by the thread named, as on Linux: a thread of the
same process that has it shares the memory reached.
The check is made at the attach and the open: a descriptor opened before
keeps working, and a tracer that attached before keeps its tracee, whose
memory it reads while the tracee is dumpable or the requesting thread
privileged, where Linux judges the credentials of the attach.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std capget capset
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/musl/capmusl.c
gives up every capability and checks what is refused, and that an exec
gives them back; it checks the rules of the inheritable set, and that an
exec under no_new_privs keeps an ambient capability, and so does the exec
of user 1000 in a user namespace of its own, as bubblewrap's
.Fl -cap-add
needs when an ordinary user runs it.
.Pa tests/musl/sandboxmusl.c
keeps
.Dv CAP_SYS_ADMIN
alone and then
.Dv CAP_CHOWN
alone, and checks that a capability dropped from the bounding set does not
come back through a fork or an exec.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The header or the data cannot be read or written.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The version is not one of the three.
The kernel writes
.Dv _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_3
into the header, and
.Fn capget
with a null
.Fa datap
returns 0.
The pid given to
.Fn capget
is negative.
.It Bq Er EPERM
.Fn capset
was given a pid other than 0 and the caller's, a permitted capability the
thread does not have, an effective one outside the permitted set, or an
inheritable one not already inheritable that is outside the bounding set
or, without
.Dv CAP_SETPCAP ,
one the thread does not hold.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
The pid given to
.Fn capget
names no thread.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr prctl 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
.Sh STANDARDS
Linux keeps the effective set apart from the permitted one and checks
each capability on its own.
A thread whose user IDs are not root holds no capability here but those
an exec gave it from the ambient set.
.Dv PR_SET_KEEPCAPS
and the securebits are not supported.
